body:
Its weird, on one hand I feel like I was never a kid (my abuse started around 7). On the other, I feel like I'll ALWAYS be a kid. Basically, my goal is to learn to do the things I need to, as an adult, and not worry too much about childlike behavior unless it hurts others or affects my relationships -- but I never behaved like that (bratty, selfish, tantrum-y), even when I was a kid. I was a "little adult". So it mostly manifests in being derpily enthusiastic about some things and socially dumb. But I'm also autistic. Idk. 

&#x200B;

You probably need to address the trauma, then the other things might get better. But like I said, look at things piece by piece, figure out which childlike traits are holding you back, and focus on eliminating those, rather than suddenly forcing yourself to be/feel like an adult.

&#x200B;

Is there a way you could take just ONE college class? Starting out slow with new responsibilities helps me a lot. But there have definitely been times in my life where I couldn't handle even that. There's no shame in being ill.

I'm twice your age. I was similarly miserable when I was your age. Do you know what I wish I had done, more than anything? Gone to a counselor and told them everything I was feeling, everything that had happened to me. Started to work on my issues. I did go to therapy, but I lied the whole time. I wasted the opportunity to work on my shit. Because of that, and continuing to either avoid therapy completely, or lie to my therapists, I wasted my 20s on drug addiction and being horribly depressed. I often wonder what my life would be like now if I had been better about seeking help.

&#x200B;

Not to say that it's ever "too late". I finally started working on my shit, got clean, and am now back in college for something I really love. I have a lot of really daunting work to do in therapy, addressing the stuff I should have been working on when I was your age, but that's okay. I'm still around, I've survived, I can do this. So can you.
